# The module allowlist: the only drivers `build/mkiso.sh` keeps, and the only ones `build/init`
# loads. Everything else in Debian's 4229-module tree is deleted and `modules.dep` is regenerated
# from what is left, so a module that is not named here or pulled in as a dependency of one is not
# in the image at all.
#
# `docs/boot-pipeline.md` states the claim this file makes true, and states it at ABSENCE strength:
# the Alpine predecessor built `CONFIG_MODULES=n` and the mechanism did not exist; Debian's stock
# kernel has every one of these as `m` and this project does not control that config.
#
# ONE NAME PER LINE, the module name and not the filename — `hid_generic`, not `hid-generic.ko.xz`.
# `depmod` resolves dependencies, so a module needed only because something here needs it does not
# belong in this file: `usbcore`, `usb_common`, `videodev` and the input core all arrive that way.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THERE IS NO GRAPHICS DRIVER HERE, and that is a change from what this document used to say.
#
# `docs/boot-pipeline.md` named `i915`, `amdgpu`, `nouveau` and `simpledrm`. Measured against the
# pinned kernel's own config, three facts moved that decision:
#
#   * `CONFIG_DRM_SIMPLEDRM is not set`. The module does not exist in Debian's kernel, so a quarter
#     of the old allowlist named a file that was never going to be found.
#   * `CONFIG_FB_EFI=y` and `CONFIG_FB_VESA=y`. **Both firmware framebuffers are built in**, so the
#     console mechanism the document actually names — fbcon over a firmware framebuffer — needs no
#     module on either firmware path. UEFI gets `efifb` at the GOP's resolution; BIOS gets `vesafb`
#     at the `vga=791` mode.
#   * The three DRM drivers need firmware blobs this image does not ship. Loading one would take
#     the framebuffer away from a driver that is working and hand it to one that may not come up —
#     trading a console that is guaranteed for a console that is faster, on an appliance that draws
#     QR codes and text.
#
# They also cost about 70 MiB of the 98 MiB module tree, which is the least interesting of the
# three reasons and the only one that would have been reversible.

# --- USB host controllers -----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Without one of these there is no USB bus at all: no camera, no external keyboard, and nothing for
# `authorized_default=0` to close. They are not dependencies of `usbhid` — a host controller and a
# device driver are unrelated in the module graph — so they have to be named.
#
# ALL FOUR STAY, and that is a decision rather than an oversight. The one machine this image has
# booted on is xHCI-only, so three of these four resolve to nothing there and `docs/roadmap.md` M3
# asked whether to narrow the list to what that machine needs. Narrowing it would turn "the image
# boots on a machine with USB" into "the image boots on a machine with xHCI", retiring every laptop
# older than about 2010 — which is precisely the hardware most likely to be sitting unused in a
# drawer, and an offline signer's most natural host. The saving is a few hundred KiB against a tree
# already cut from 98 MiB, and that is the least interesting reason to keep them.
xhci_pci
ehci_pci
ohci_pci
uhci_hcd

# --- Human interface devices ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `usbhid` is the transport and `hid_generic` is the driver that actually binds a *USB* keyboard.
# Naming only the first is the mistake that produces a machine with an enumerated keyboard and no
# keys.
#
# THIS FILE IS NOT WHERE A LAPTOP'S OWN KEYBOARD COMES FROM, and reading it as if it were is how the
# repository came to describe input as a USB story when the only machine it has ever booted on used
# neither module for keystrokes. On that machine the keyboard sits behind a built-in i8042
# controller: `CONFIG_SERIO_I8042=y`, `CONFIG_SERIO_LIBPS2=y` and `CONFIG_KEYBOARD_ATKBD=y` are all
# `=y` in the pinned kernel, so they are in the image whatever this file says and cannot be removed
# by editing it. `build/verify.py` asserts they stay `=y`, because a future Debian flipping
# `KEYBOARD_ATKBD` to `m` would delete the keyboard from the image and every other assertion here
# would still pass.
#
# Input reaches the application through the **vt keyboard handler**, not `/dev/input/event*`:
# `CONFIG_INPUT_EVDEV=m` and evdev is not named here, so it is deleted. Nothing needs it.
#
# `cros_ec_keyb` is DELIBERATELY ABSENT. A Chromebook that routes its keyboard through the embedded
# controller rather than i8042 would boot this image and have no keys at all. Adding the driver for
# a machine class nobody has booted is the pattern behind all three faults `docs/roadmap.md` M3
# records — a claim the repository stated and never checked — so it is a post-gate row there, not a
# line here.
usbhid
hid_generic

# --- The camera ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# UVC, and nothing else. `aobs/adapters/real/frames.py` reads raw frames from `/dev/video*` through
# `ioctl`; `videodev` arrives as a dependency and is what creates that node.
uvcvideo
